; ============================================================
; TITLE:    Towers of Hanoi in Text
; CATEGORY: Recursion
; LEVEL:    Intermediate
; TAGS:     recursion, hanoi, lists, puzzles, call stack
; SUMMARY:  Solves the Towers of Hanoi recursively and prints the three pegs as rows of text after every move.
; ============================================================
;
; WHAT IT DOES
;   There are three pegs, A, B and C. A tower of discs sits on A, the
;   biggest at the bottom. Move the whole tower to C, one disc at a
;   time, never putting a bigger disc on a smaller one.
;   The trick: to move 4 discs from A to C, first move the top 3 out of
;   the way onto B, then move the big disc to C, then move the 3 from
;   B onto C. Moving 3 discs is the same puzzle, only smaller.
;   Each peg is kept as a list of disc sizes, top disc first, and the
;   pegs are printed after each move so you can follow along.
;
; HOW TO USE
;   1. Press Run and scroll through the moves in the text window.
;   2. Put a breakpoint on the line  movedisc :from :to  and run.
;      Open the Call Stack. Every HANOI on it is in the middle of its
;      own three-part plan. Click each one and look at :n, :from and
;      :to in the Variables panel.
;   3. Add a watch for  :moves  and press F5 to step move by move.
;
; CONCEPTS YOU WILL LEARN
;   * solving a big problem by trusting the same procedure on smaller ones
;   * lists as stacks with FIRST, BUTFIRST and FPUT
;   * THING and MAKE with a name held in a variable
;
; THINGS TO TRY
;   * Change start.puzzle 4 to 3 or 5. How many moves does each need?
;     Can you find the pattern?
;   * Make it say which disc is moving, not just which pegs.
;   * Add a check in MOVEDISC that complains if a big disc ever lands
;     on a smaller one. (It never will!)
; ============================================================

; Each peg is a global variable named A, B or C holding a list,
; for example [1 2 3 4] means disc 1 (the smallest) is on top.
to start.puzzle :n
  make "a iseq 1 :n
  make "b []
  make "c []
  make "moves 0
  print (sentence [Start with] :n [discs on peg A.])
  show.pegs
  hanoi :n "a "c "b
  print (sentence [Finished in] :moves [moves.])
end

; Move :n discs from peg :from to peg :to, using :spare to park them.
to hanoi :n :from :to :spare
  if :n = 0 [stop]
  hanoi :n - 1 :from :spare :to
  movedisc :from :to
  hanoi :n - 1 :spare :to :from
end

; Take the top disc off one peg and put it on another. :from holds
; the NAME of the peg, so THING :from gets the list stored there.
to movedisc :from :to
  localmake "disc first thing :from
  make :from butfirst thing :from
  make :to fput :disc thing :to
  make "moves :moves + 1
  print (sentence (word "Move "| | :moves ":) "disc :disc [from] uppercase :from [to] uppercase :to)
  show.pegs
end

to show.pegs
  peg "A :a
  peg "B :b
  peg "C :c
  print []
end

; A disc of size 3 prints as ===3===, so bigger discs look wider. The
; list is reversed so the bottom disc comes first, on the left: each
; peg looks like a tower lying on its side.
to peg :name :discs
  type word :name "|: |
  foreach reverse :discs [type (word bar ? ? bar ? "| |)]
  print "
end

to bar :n
  if :n = 0 [output "]
  output word "= bar :n - 1
end

start.puzzle 4
